= Semiconductor =

== Overview ==

{{:Semiconductor/Simple}}

The introductory explanation gives the essential meaning of the subject. The rest of this article distinguishes materials, devices and systems and provides routes to [[Semiconductor/Simple|a beginner-friendly explanation]] and an independent [[Semiconductor/Technical|technical account]] of the underlying physics and engineering.

== What the name refers to ==

A '''semiconductor material''' is a physical substance with electronic states and charge transport that can be shaped by doping, fields, light and interfaces. A '''semiconductor device''' is an engineered structure using such a material; examples include diodes, transistors, photodetectors and solar cells. An '''integrated circuit''' contains many such devices and electrical interconnections. The '''semiconductor industry''' is the network of organizations, processes and equipment used to design, manufacture, package and distribute devices. These are related subjects but not one interchangeable definition.<ref name="openstaxcore">OpenStax, ''University Physics, Volume 3'', [https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/9-6-semiconductors-and-doping §9.6 “Semiconductors and Doping”] and [https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/9-7-semiconductor-devices §9.7 “Semiconductor Devices”].</ref>

The subject can be studied through quantum and solid-state physics, chemistry and materials science, device physics, electrical engineering, manufacturing, and computing. Each examines a different level of the same physical system; merely describing a processor does not explain carrier transport, and understanding a band gap does not specify a computer architecture.

