== Governing physical distinctions ==

In crystalline semiconductors, occupied and available electron energy states form bands. A '''band gap''' is a range without the relevant extended electron states. Electrons and positively behaving holes can transport electrical charge, while doping, light, temperature and applied fields can alter carrier distributions. In an engineered p–n junction, spatial charge and internal fields affect transport; in a MOSFET, gate voltage controls a channel. Semiconductor theory is therefore both a study of material properties and a basis for designing devices.<ref name="mitcore">Jesús del Alamo, ''Integrated Microelectronic Devices'', MIT OpenCourseWare 6.720J (Spring 2007), [https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-720j-integrated-microelectronic-devices-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/ lecture notes] on charge carriers, junctions and MOSFETs.</ref>

The related [[Related to::Electric current|electric current]] article explains charge flow as a measurable electrical quantity; semiconductor physics supplies material-dependent mechanisms for that flow. Semiconductor device behavior also connects to [[Related to::Computer architecture|computer architecture]] through physical switching networks and to [[Related to::Energy conversion|energy conversion]] through photovoltaic and optoelectronic systems. These broad associations do not assert that a semiconductor literally implements an architecture or that its material is a constituent of every energy converter.

== Materials, evidence and engineering limits ==

Silicon and germanium are elemental semiconductors. Gallium arsenide, silicon carbide and gallium nitride are examples of compound semiconductors. The latter can offer useful optical or high-field properties in appropriate devices, but suitability depends on crystal structure, defect populations, contacts, interfaces, heat removal, geometry and operating conditions. Amorphous and organic semiconductors require care because the simple crystalline band picture does not describe every transport regime.

A material should not be classified as “better” solely because of a wider band gap, higher mobility, finer manufacturing node or its use in a more advanced computer. A responsible comparison specifies the intended application, operating limits and measurements. The quantitative conditions and known failure modes are developed in [[Semiconductor/Technical#Model_selection_and_unresolved_engineering_limits|Technical: model selection and limits]].

== Choose a reading depth ==

For a low-prerequisite explanation with examples, read [[Semiconductor/Simple|Simple]]. For the mathematical treatment of electron statistics, charge transport, junctions, transistor models and model limits, read [[Semiconductor/Technical|Technical]]. The technical article can be read from its beginning without first reading the simpler explanation.

